course: dictionary
lesson: 14850
title: Randomly: no plan, just chance
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Randomly» is an adverb. It tells you how something happens. If something happens randomly, there was no plan, pattern or reason behind it. «The teacher picked names randomly from the pot.» The teacher did not choose the names in order or because of who was tallest. Nobody could know which name would come out next.
explain: Randomly also means by chance. When every choice has the same chance of being picked, the choice is random. «Each counter in the bag was picked randomly, so every colour had an equal chance.» In maths, a fair dice gives random results, because you cannot predict which number will land face up.
explain: Look at the words around the word, which is its context. «Sam randomly picked a card» tells you Sam did not look for a special card. The clue is the word picked: he chose one without a plan. Try your meaning in the sentence: «Sam picked a card without a plan» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: People also use randomly in everyday speech, to mean unexpectedly. «He randomly started singing in the queue.» Nobody planned it, and nobody expected it. Phrases you may hear are «chosen randomly», «randomly picked» and «at random», and they all mean the same idea: by chance, without a plan.
fact: «Randomly» means without a plan, pattern or reason.
fact: «Randomly» also means by chance, so that every choice has the same chance of being picked.
fact: «At random» means the same as «randomly»: «She picked a book at random.»
fact: In a fair test, scientists may choose samples randomly, so that no one can pick the best ones on purpose.
fact: Games use random numbers, so you cannot predict what will happen next.
exercise: «Ms Lee picked a name randomly from the hat.» What does randomly mean here? | *With no plan, so any name could be chosen | Very slowly | After a long think | With a loud shout
exercise: Which is a random choice? | *Rolling a dice to decide who goes first | Always choosing the same card from the pile | Putting the biggest sweet in your own hand | Picking the tallest child to go first every time
exercise: «Tom chose the cards randomly, without looking.» Find and copy the word that tells you how Tom chose. | Tom | cards | *randomly | chose
test: «The spinner landed on blue, but it was random, so nobody could have known.» What does random mean in this sentence? | Happening only in the morning | *Happening by chance, with no plan | Happening very quickly | Happening on purpose, after careful thought
test: «He randomly started singing in the middle of the lesson.» What does randomly mean here? | Carefully, after a long plan | Slowly, one word at a time | *Unexpectedly, with no warning | Quietly, so nobody heard
exam: «Each name was picked randomly, so every child had an equal chance.» Why does the author use randomly? | To show that the children were tired | To show that the teacher was angry | *To show that the names were chosen by chance, with no plan | To show that the names were all the same
